{:edb/db-before-t 2548 :edb/db-after-t 2549 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535861 50 #inst "2026-10-15T02:06:42.598Z" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186052635 1078 #edb/ref 17592186052640 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186054688 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534064 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186054711 1038 #edb/ref 17592186533990 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186055233 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186055233 1071 "![](images/b7/b75586450ddee4f682f02539e02462d0aa5f88830b2989cc7cc2f4f6df635ade.png)\n\nFind $lim_(x → - 1)(5 + 7x)^{3}(f(x) - x)^{4}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above." 13194139535861 17592186045417 false] [17592186055233 1071 "![](images/b7/b75586450ddee4f682f02539e02462d0aa5f88830b2989cc7cc2f4f6df635ade.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}(5+7x)^{3}(f(x)-x)^{4}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above." 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186055233 1074 "From the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)=0.$ \n\nFrom the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}(5+7x)^{3}(f(x)-x)^{4}={lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}(5+7x)^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}(f(x)-x)^{4}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of each factor, separately.\n\nApplying the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}(5+7x)^{3} & ={({lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}(5+7x))}^{3} \\\\  & =(5+7\\cdot (-1))^{3} \\\\  & =(-2)^{3} \\\\  & =-8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}(f(x)-x)^{4} & ={({lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}(f(x)-x))}^{4} \\\\  & =(0-(-1))^{4} \\\\  & ={1}^{4} \\\\  & =1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}(5+7x)^{3}(f(x)-x)^{4} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}(5+7x)^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}(f(x)-x)^{4} \\\\  & =(-8)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =-8\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186064236 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534065 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186064244 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534039 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186064310 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534031 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186064310 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534079 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186064310 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534090 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186064311 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534067 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186068747 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534018 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186068799 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534074 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186068799 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534098 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186068800 1038 #edb/ref 17592186533998 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186073820 1038 #edb/ref 17592186533988 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186073885 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534056 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186073896 1038 #edb/ref 17592186533991 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186078566 1038 #edb/ref 17592186533982 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186078566 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534005 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186083241 1039 "Solving Rationals Equations by Factoring a Quadratic Denominator With No Constant Term" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186083241 1039 "Solving a Rational Equations by Factoring a Quadratic Denominator With No Constant Term" 13194139535861 17592186045417 false] [17592186083242 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534024 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186083412 1050 720.0 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186094771 1075 #edb/ref 17592186094772 13194139535861 17592186045417 false] [17592186094771 1075 #edb/ref 17592186094773 13194139535861 17592186045417 false] [17592186094771 1075 #edb/ref 17592186094774 13194139535861 17592186045417 false] [17592186094771 1075 #edb/ref 17592186094775 13194139535861 17592186045417 false] [17592186094771 1075 #edb/ref 17592186094776 13194139535861 17592186045417 false] [17592186094771 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533161 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186094771 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533327 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186094771 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533554 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186094771 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533595 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186094771 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533677 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186094771 1078 #edb/ref 17592186533161 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186096538 1071 "![](images/e9/e933e484b931ee54b345a8e7af3b0e44f80a8b019e3e401007ad4987192e33be.png)\n\nFind the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx = t^2 - 1, \\qquad y = t^3 - 4t\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $t \\in [-2,0].$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186096538 1071 "![](images/e9/e933e484b931ee54b345a8e7af3b0e44f80a8b019e3e401007ad4987192e33be.png)\n\nFind the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n$x = t^2 - 1, \\qquad y = t^3 - 4t$\nand the $x$-axis for $t \\in [-2,0]$." 13194139535861 17592186045417 false] [17592186096538 1074 "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx = x(t), \\qquad y = y(t), \\qquad t_1 \\leq t \\leq t_2\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n\\int_{t_1}^{t_2} y \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} \\:\\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2} - 1) \\\\\n&= 2t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{t_{1}}^{t_{2}}y\\frac{dx}{dt}dt &= \\int_{-2}^{0}(t^{3} - 4t)(2t)dt \\\\\n&= 2\\int_{-2}^{0}t^{2}(t^{2} - 4)dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a negative number since\n\n$$\ny\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} \\leq 0\n$$\n\nfor all $t \\in [-2,0]$.\n\nTherefore, the area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= -2\\int_{-2}^{0}t^{2}(t^{2} - 4)dt \\\\\n&= 2\\int_{-2}^{0}t^{2}(4 - t^{2})dt \\\\\n&= 2\\int_{-2}^{0}(4t^{2} - t^{4})dt \\\\\n&= 8\\int_{-2}^{0}t^{2}dt - 2\\int_{-2}^{0}t^{4}dt \\\\\n&= 8 \\cdot \\left.\\frac{t^{3}}{3}\\right|_{-2}^{0} - 2 \\cdot \\left.\\frac{t^{5}}{5}\\right|_{-2}^{0} \\\\\n&= 8(\\frac{0^{3}}{3} - ((-2)^{3})/(3)) - 2(\\frac{0^{5}}{5} - ((-2)^{5})/(5)) \\\\\n&= 8(\\frac{8}{3}) - 2(\\frac{32}{5}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{64}{3} - \\frac{64}{5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{128}{15}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 false] [17592186096538 1074 "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx = x(t), \\qquad\ny = y(t), \\qquad\nt_1 \\leq t \\leq t_2\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n\\int_{t_1}^{t_2} y \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} \\:\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}({t}^{3}-4t)(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{t}^{2}({t}^{2}-4)\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a negative number since $y\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} \\leq 0$ for all $t \\in [-2,0].$\n\nTherefore, the area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =-2{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{t}^{2}({t}^{2}-4)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{t}^{2}(4-{t}^{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-2}^{0}(4{t}^{2}-{t}^{4})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t-2{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}\\,{|}_{-2}^{0}-2\\cdot \\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}\\,{|}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =8(\\frac{{0}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{(-2)^{3}}{3})-2(\\frac{{0}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{(-2)^{5}}{5}) \\\\  & =8(\\frac{8}{3})-2(\\frac{32}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{3}-\\frac{64}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{128}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186096561 1071 "![](images/3d/3dd84b2e84056a4e6c71f7b76534f5a6f1a46132ea5da5ed8edb2a514b9ea6b4.png)\n\nFind an integral expression for the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx = e^{-t^2}, \\qquad y = t^3,\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $0 \\leq t \\leq \\sqrt{2} \\,?$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186096561 1071 "![](images/3d/3dd84b2e84056a4e6c71f7b76534f5a6f1a46132ea5da5ed8edb2a514b9ea6b4.png)\n\nFind an integral expression for the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n$x = e^{-t^2}, \\qquad y = t^3$,\nand the $x$-axis for $0 \\leq t \\leq \\sqrt{2} \\,$?" 13194139535861 17592186045417 false] [17592186096561 1074 "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx = x(t), \\qquad y = y(t), \\qquad t_1 \\leq t \\leq t_2\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n\\int_{t_1}^{t_2} y \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} \\:\\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(e^{-t^{2}}) \\\\\n&=-2te^{-t^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{t_{1}}^{t_{2}}y\\frac{dx}{dt}dt &= \\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}t^{3}(-2te^{-t^{2}})dt \\\\\n&=-2\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}t^{4}e^{-t^{2}}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a negative number since\n\n$$\ny\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} \\leq 0\n$$\n\nfor all $t \\in [0, \\sqrt{2}]$.\n\nTherefore, the area is given by\n\n$$\nA = -\\left(-2 \\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}} t^4 e^{-t^2} \\, \\textrm{d}t \\right) = 2 \\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}} t^4 e^{-t^2} \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 false] [17592186096561 1074 "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx = x(t), \\qquad\ny = y(t), \\qquad\nt_1 \\leq t \\leq t_2\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n\\int_{t_1}^{t_2} y \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} \\:\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-{t}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-2t{e}^{-{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{t}^{3}(-2t{e}^{-{t}^{2}})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{t}^{4}{e}^{-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a negative number since $y\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} \\leq 0$ for all $t \\in [0, \\sqrt{2}].$\n\nTherefore, the area is given by\n\n$$\nA = -\\left( -2 \\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}} t^4 e^{-t^2} \\, \\textrm{d}t \\right) = 2 \\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}} t^4 e^{-t^2} \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186096731 1038 #edb/ref 17592186533985 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186096731 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534025 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186096731 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534040 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186096731 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534049 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186096731 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534062 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186096758 1038 #edb/ref 17592186533987 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186096758 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534014 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186096758 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534021 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186096758 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534099 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186096821 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534044 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186096851 1050 720.0 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186097133 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186097133 1071 "![](images/3b/3bde5b93aa28654977fdb55b0b2808fde2fb2f54ba33a6e9a2a4281b6966682f.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx={t}^{4}-2{t}^{2},\\,y={t}^{3}-1\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $t\\in [1,\\sqrt{2}]?$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186097133 1071 "![](images/3b/3bde5b93aa28654977fdb55b0b2808fde2fb2f54ba33a6e9a2a4281b6966682f.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n$x = t^{4} - 2t^{2}, y = t^{3} - 1$\nand the $x$-axis for $t ∈ [1, \\sqrt{2}]$?" 13194139535861 17592186045417 false] [17592186097133 1074 "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=y(t),\\,{t}_{1}\\le t\\le {t}_{2}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}-2{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =4{t}^{3}-4t \\\\  & =4t({t}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}({t}^{3}-1)4t({t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}t({t}^{3}-1)({t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a positive number since $y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\ge 0$ for all $t\\in [1,\\sqrt{2}].$\n\nTherefore, the area is given by\n\n$$\nA=4{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}t({t}^{3}-1)({t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186097612 1061 2136 13194139535861 17592186045417 false] [17592186097612 1061 630055 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186097613 1061 2138 13194139535861 17592186045417 false] [17592186097613 1061 630060 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186097653 1061 27495 13194139535861 17592186045417 false] [17592186097653 1061 630057 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186097747 1061 45599 13194139535861 17592186045417 false] [17592186097747 1061 630056 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186097798 1064 "![](images/d8/d8f08b1cc474cfee367ccf52e2299deaeec146ef7a7a860f64a75f7f7f25cb0b.png)\n\nConsider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=t^3-1, \\qquad\ny=2t-t^3, \\qquad\nt \\in  (-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$\n\nThe shaded area shown in the diagram is the finite region bounded between the curve, the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines formed by $x$-axis and the points on the curve corresponding to $t_1=0$ to $t_2=1,$ as shown. \n\nWe know that the area of the curve is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\int_{a}^{b} y \\:\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is defined parametrically, we need to change the variable of integration from $x$ to $t$ using the change of variables formula, given by\n\n$$\nA = \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} y(t) \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm d t}\\:\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nNow, in this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-1)=3{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the change of variables formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y(t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-{t}^{3})\\cdot 3{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6{t}^{3}-3{t}^{5}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t-3{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{5}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\cdot {\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{1}-3\\cdot {\\frac{{t}^{6}}{6}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =6(\\frac{1}{4}-0)-3(\\frac{1}{6}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shaded area shown in the picture has an area of $1$ square unit." 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186097798 1064 "![](images/d8/d8f08b1cc474cfee367ccf52e2299deaeec146ef7a7a860f64a75f7f7f25cb0b.png)\n\nConsider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=t^3-1, \\qquad y=2t-t^3, \\qquad t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)\n$$\n\nThe shaded area shown in the diagram is the finite region bounded between the curve, the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines formed by $x$-axis and the points on the curve corresponding to $t_1=0$ to $t_2=1$, as shown.\n\nWe know that the area of the curve is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\int_{a}^{b} y \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nSince our curve is defined parametrically, we need to change the variable of integration from $x$ to $t$ using the change of variables formula, given by\n\n$$\nA = \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} y(t) \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm d t}\\:\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nNow, in this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt} = \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{3} - 1) = 3t^{2}\n$$\n\nApplying the change of variables formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\int_{t_{1}}^{t_{2}}y(t)\\frac{dx}{dt}dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}(2t - t^{3}) \\cdot 3t^{2}dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}6t^{3} - 3t^{5}dt \\\\\n&= 6\\int_{0}^{1}t^{3}dt - 3\\int_{0}^{1}t^{5}dt \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot \\left.\\frac{t^{4}}{4}\\right|_{0}^{1} - 3 \\cdot \\left.\\frac{t^{6}}{6}\\right|_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= 6(\\frac{1}{4} - 0) - 3(\\frac{1}{6} - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2} - \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shaded area shown in the picture has an area of $1$ square unit." 13194139535861 17592186045417 false] [17592186097915 1064 "In general, the area between the $x$-axis and the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx = x(t), \\qquad y = y(t), \\qquad t_1 \\leq t \\leq t_2\n$$\n\nis given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n\\int_{t_1}^{t_2} y \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} \\:\\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When applying this formula, we assume that the chosen parametrization traverses the curve only once.\n- It's possible that this integral gives a negative value for the area even when the corresponding region lies entirely above the $x$-axis! To see why, note that if the curve lies above the $x$-axis, we must have $y(t) > 0$. However, if the curve is parameterized so that it is traversed from right to left, then $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} < 0$, and we have\n$y\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} < 0$\nand thus, our integral turns out to be negative.\n- To ensure that we get an unsigned area, we take the absolute value of the integral.\n- In general, care must be taken when asked to compute the area bounded by a parametric curve when $y\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}$ changes sign over the domain of integration. However, we won't consider that scenario here.\n\nLet's see another example." 13194139535861 17592186045417 false] [17592186097915 1064 "In general, the area between the $x$-axis and the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx = x(t), \\qquad\ny = y(t), \\qquad\nt_1 \\leq t \\leq t_2\n$$\n\nis given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n\\int_{t_1}^{t_2} y \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} \\:\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When applying this formula, we assume that the chosen parametrization traverses the curve only once.\n\n- It's possible that this integral gives a negative value for the area even when the corresponding region lies entirely above the $x$-axis! \n\nTo see why, note that if the curve lies above the $x$-axis, we must have $y(t) > 0.$ However, if the curve is parameterized so that it is traversed from right to left, then $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} < 0,$ and we have\n\n$$\ny\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} < 0\n$$\n\nand thus, our integral turns out to be negative.\n\n- To ensure that we get an unsigned area, we take the absolute value of the integral.\n\n- In general, care must be taken when asked to compute the area bounded by a parametric curve when $y\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}$ changes sign over the domain of integration. However, we won't consider that scenario here.\n\nLet's see another example." 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186100443 1078 #edb/ref 17592186100448 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186100583 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{n = 4}^\\infty  {\\dfrac{3}{{n(n + 1)}}}.$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186100583 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{n = 4}^\\infty {\\dfrac{3}{{n(n + 1)}}}$." 13194139535861 17592186045417 false] [17592186100583 1074 "First, note that the series can be written as\n\n$$\n3\\sum\\limits_{n = 4}^\\infty  {\\dfrac{1}{{n(n + 1)}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing partial fractions, we can write the series as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle 3\\sum\\limits_{n = 4}^\\infty  \\left(\\dfrac 1 n - \\dfrac 1 {n+1}\\right).\n$$\n\nComputing the first few partial sums, starting with $n=4,$ gives\n\n$\\require{cancel}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{4} & =3[(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{5})] \\\\ {s}_{5} & =3[(\\frac{1}{4}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}}-\\frac{1}{6})] \\\\ {s}_{6} & =3[(\\frac{1}{4}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}}-\\frac{1}{7})] \\\\ {s}_{7} & =3[(\\frac{1}{4}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}}-\\frac{1}{8})].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in each partial sum, we always end up canceling all terms except for the first and last terms. Therefore, the $N$th partial sum is\n\n$$\ns_N = 3\\left[\\dfrac 1 4 - \\dfrac{1}{N+1}\\right].\n$$\n\nFinally, we can determine the sum of the series by taking the limit of $s_N\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=4}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{n(n+1)} & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }{s}_{N} \\\\  & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }3[\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{N+1}] \\\\  & =3[\\frac{1}{4}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186100583 1074 "First, note that the series can be written as\n\n$$\n3\\sum\\limits_{n = 4}^\\infty {\\dfrac{1}{{n(n + 1)}}}\n$$\n\nUsing partial fractions, we can write the series as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle 3\\sum\\limits_{n = 4}^\\infty \\left(\\dfrac 1 n - \\dfrac 1 {n+1}\\right)\n$$\n\nComputing the first few partial sums, starting with $n=4$, gives\n\n$\\require{cancel}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ns_{4} &= 3[(\\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{5})] \\\\\ns_{5} &= 3[(\\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{5}) + (\\frac{1}{5} - \\frac{1}{6})] \\\\\ns_{6} &= 3[(\\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{5}) + (\\frac{1}{5} - \\frac{1}{6}) + (\\frac{1}{6} - \\frac{1}{7})] \\\\\ns_{7} &= 3[(\\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{5}) + (\\frac{1}{5} - \\frac{1}{6}) + (\\frac{1}{6} - \\frac{1}{7}) + (\\frac{1}{7} - \\frac{1}{8})]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in each partial sum, we always end up canceling all terms except for the first and last terms. Therefore, the $N$th partial sum is\n\n$$\ns_N = 3\\left[\\dfrac 1 4 - \\dfrac{1}{N+1}\\right]\n$$\n\nFinally, we can determine the sum of the series by taking the limit of $s_N$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(n = 4)^(∞)(3)/(n(n + 1)) &= lim_(N → ∞)s_{N} \\\\\n&= lim_(N → ∞)3[\\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{N + 1}] \\\\\n&= 3[\\frac{1}{4} - 0] \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 false] [17592186100584 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{n = 1}^\\infty   \\dfrac{1}{(n+2)(n+3)}.$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186100584 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{n = 1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{(n+2)(n+3)}$." 13194139535861 17592186045417 false] [17592186100584 1074 "Using partial fractions, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{(n+2)(n+3)} = \\dfrac{1}{n+2} - \\dfrac{1}{n+3}\n$$\n\nSo, our series can be written as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{n = 1}^\\infty \\left(\\dfrac{1}{n+2} - \\dfrac{1}{n+3}\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's now compute the first few partial sums:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ns_{1} &= (\\frac{1}{3} - \\frac{1}{4}) \\\\\ns_{2} &= (\\frac{1}{3} - \\frac{1}{4}) + (\\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{5}) \\\\\ns_{3} &= (\\frac{1}{3} - \\frac{1}{4}) + (\\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{5}) + (\\frac{1}{5} - \\frac{1}{6}) \\\\\ns_{4} &= (\\frac{1}{3} - \\frac{1}{4}) + (\\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{5}) + (\\frac{1}{5} - \\frac{1}{6}) + (\\frac{1}{6} - \\frac{1}{7})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in each partial sum, we always end up canceling all terms except for the first and last terms. Therefore, the $N$th partial sum is\n\n$$\ns_N = \\dfrac{1}{3} - \\dfrac{1}{N+3}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can determine the sum of the series by taking the limit of $s_N$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(n = 1)^