en as\n\n$$\n8{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}+6n+8}.\n$$\n\nWe can factor the denominator and use partial fractions to show that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}+6n+8}=\\frac{1}{(n+2)(n+4)}=\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{n+2}-\\frac{1}{n+4}).\n$$\n\nSo, our series can be written as\n\n$$\n8{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{n+2}-\\frac{1}{n+4})=4{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }(\\frac{1}{n+2}-\\frac{1}{n+4}).\n$$\n\nLet's now compute the first few partial sums:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{2} & =4[(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{6})] \\\\ {s}_{3} & =4[(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{6})+(\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{7})] \\\\ {s}_{4} & =4[(\\frac{1}{4}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}})+(\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{7})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}}-\\frac{1}{8})] \\\\ {s}_{5} & =4[(\\frac{1}{4}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}})+(\\frac{1}{5}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}}-\\frac{1}{8})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}}-\\frac{1}{9})] \\\\ {s}_{6} & =4[(\\frac{1}{4}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}})+(\\frac{1}{5}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{8}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}}-\\frac{1}{9})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{8}}-\\frac{1}{10})]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe notice that all of the terms cancel except $\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{5},$ and the $-\\frac{1}{N+3}$ and  $-\\frac{1}{N+4}$ terms. Therefore, the $N$th partial sum is\n\n$$\n{s}_{N}=4[\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{N+3}-\\frac{1}{N+4}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we can determine the sum of the series by taking the limit of ${s}_{N}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{{n}^{2}+6n+8} & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }⁡{s}_{N} \\\\  & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }⁡4[\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{N+3}-\\frac{1}{N+4}] \\\\  & =4{lim}_{N\\to \\infty }⁡[\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{N+3}-\\frac{1}{N+4}] \\\\  & =4[\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{5}-0-0] \\\\  & =4[\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{5}] \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{9}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186101819 1061 41059 13194139535861 175921860