ual to zero. So, we solve for the value of $k$ that makes the right-hand side equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{k - 10} &= 0 \\\\\nk - 10 &= 0 \\\\\nk &= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=10$ will give only one solution to the equation." 13194139535861 17592186045417 false] [17592186110348 1074 "First, we solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2}+10 & =k \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =k-10 \\\\ \\sqrt{(x+3)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{k-10} \\\\ |x+3| & =\\sqrt{k-10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation has only one solution if the right-hand side is equal to zero. So, we solve for the value of $k$ that makes the right-hand side equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{k-10} & =0 \\\\ k-10 & =0 \\\\ k & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=10$ will give only one solution to the equation." 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186110349 1071 "Given that $g^2 -18g + 81 = 0$, what are the values of $g$?" 13194139535861 17592186045417 false] [17592186110349 1071 "Given that $g^2 -18g + 81 = 0$, what are the values of $g?$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186110349 1074 "First, notice that the left-hand side is a perfect square. It just needs to be factored:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng^{2} - 18g + 81 &= 0 \\\\\ng^{2} - 2 \\cdot 9 \\cdot g + 9^{2} &= 0 \\\\\n(g - 9)^{2} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{(g - 9)^{2}} &= \\sqrt{0} \\\\\n\\mid g - 9 \\mid &= 0 \\\\\ng - 9 &= 0 \\\\\ng &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 false] [17592186110349 1074 "First, notice that the left-hand side is a perfect square. It just needs to be factored:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{2}-18g+81 & =0 \\\\ {g}^{2}-2\\cdot 9\\cdot g+{9}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (g-9)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(g-9)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{0} \\\\ |g-9| & =0 \\\\ g-9 & =0 \\\\ g & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$" 131941