 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186110613 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534038 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186110613 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534052 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186110613 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534060 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186110614 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534004 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186110614 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534042 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186110614 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534085 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186110615 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534081 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186110615 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534097 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186110817 1050 540.0 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186111183 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186111183 1071 "Solve $(2q + 1)^{2} = 0$." 13194139535861 17592186045417 false] [17592186111183 1071 "Solve $(2q+1)^{2}=0.$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186111183 1074 "We can solve this equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2q+1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{(2q+1)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{0} \\\\ |2q+1| & =0 \\\\ 2q+1 & =0 \\\\ 2q & =-1 \\\\ q & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186111919 1064 "Many quadratic equations have two solutions. However, it's also possible for a quadratic equation to have only one solution or no real solutions at all.\n\nFor example, consider the following quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n(3x-6)^2=0\n$$\n\nWe can solve this equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(3x - 6)^{2} &= 0 \\\\\n\\sqrt{(3x - 6)^{2}} &= \\sqrt{0} \\\\\n\\mid 3x - 6 \\mid &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNormally, at this stage, we would obtain two solutions by setting the inside of the absolute value equal to positive or negative $(\\pm)$ the right-hand side. However, in this case, the right-hand side is zero, and $+0$ and $-0$ are both just $0$.\n\nSo, we have only one solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid 3x - 6 \\mid &= 0 \\\\\n3x - 6 &= 0 \\\\\n3x &= 6 \\\\\nx &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 false] [17592186111919 1064 "Many quadratic equations have two solutions. However, it's also possible for a quadratic equation to have only one solution or no real solutions at all.\n\nFor example, consider the following quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n(3x-6)^2=0\n$$\n\nWe can solve this equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3x-6)^{2} & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{(3x-6)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{0} \\\\ |3x-6| & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNormally, at this stage, we would obtain two solutions by setting the inside of the absolute value equal to positive or negative $(\\pm)$ the right-hand side. However, in this case, the right-hand side is zero, and $+0$ and $-0$ are both just $0.$ \n\nSo, we have only one solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|3x-6| & =0 \\\\ 3x-6 & =0 \\\\ 3x & =6 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186111920 1064 "Let's try and solve the equation\n\n$$\n(x+1)^2=-1\n$$\n\nusing the square root method.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 1)^{2} &= -1 \\\\\n\\sqrt{(x + 1)^{2}} &= \\sqrt{-1} \\\\\n\\mid x + 1 \\mid &= \\sqrt{-1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have a problem here. We cannot take the square root of a negative number. For that reason, the equation above has *no real solutions*." 13194139535861 17592186045417 false] [17592186111920 1064 "Let's try and solve the equation $(x+1)^2=-1$ using the square root method.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{(x+1)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{-1} \\\\ |x+1| & =\\sqrt{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have a problem here. We cannot take the square root of a negative number. For that reason, the equation above has *no real solutions*." 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186117653 1078 #edb/ref 17592186117655 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186119959 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534026 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186119960 1038 #edb/ref 17592186533980 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186119961 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534043 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186120249 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186120249 1071 "$\\sqrt[3]{-8}=$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186120249 1071 "What is $\\sqrt[3]{-8}$?" 13194139535861 17592186045417 false] [17592186120249 1074 "First, notice that the number $-8$ is a perfect cube.\n\nSo, to determine the value of $\\sqrt[3]{-8},$ we express $-8$ as a perfect cube:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{-8} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{(-2)\\cdot (-2)\\cdot (-2)} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{(-2)^{3}} & = \\\\ -2 & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186124558 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534002 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186138537 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534083 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186152101 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534020 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186152199 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534051 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186152296 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534011 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186164381 1078 #edb/ref 17592186164383 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186164384 1078 #edb/ref 17592186164386 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186166919 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534013 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186166920 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534071 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186166921 1038 #edb/ref 17592186533977 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186166941 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534012 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186166943 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534066 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186167221 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186167221 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above, where the system's matrix has repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of multiplicity two. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.\n\n$\\,$ The equilibrium point (origin) is a \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\nnode." 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186167221 1071 "${x^{′}(t) = x(t) + 4y(t); y^{′}(t) =-x(t) - 3y(t)$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above, where the system's matrix has repeated eigenvalue $λ =-1$ of multiplicity two. Select the correct options below regarding the given system. The equilibrium point (origin) is a\n{{field-1}} {{field-2}}\nnode." 13194139535861 17592186045417 false] [17592186167221 1074 "The matrix of our system is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is negative, we have a $\\text{stable}$ node.\n\n- Since $A$ is not diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there is only one linearly independent eigenvector. So, we have a $\\text{degenerate}$ node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/1f/1fe96278d09a5875c2f1e7c8762a808fd9549b37508f70b129b20e311b166308.png)" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186168005 1064 "Let's sketch the phase portrait of the following linear system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=2y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWriting our system in matrix form, we have\n![](images/f5/f58fb57caaba8c5f6a110c72565c24803ac233f41b8eb2db0c5221bcf12d7fe2.svg)\nwhere ![](images/65/65298928a44147868c82da753a731e35c3aa0ad5e4b3400819d9d9f16f546a34.svg)\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=2$ with the corresponding eigenvectors $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and ![](images/69/694a89326f61bbbba7e91db5dcb607a74f99881ce16059450dbe1bbac7ee797e.svg)\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n![](images/ab/abd4dbe3156abbcd645e670fb893f93cc8765cba4b1da9bde11075e211f1eeaa.svg)\n\nNotice that for each vector ![](images/9d/9df6cb20bf3163f53850730242138ec62412ccc8af23098130d71228cac4b82a.svg) we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/71/7166c456c7912bf31d4ff2d98f4e4206537f8d01aca086fdba3c15b394db4b81.svg)\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector ![](images/23/23074d10911b113811cc92bdc88a61a6758dbd8772c21425159c5e95d30b1075.svg)\n\nSince $e^{2t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions move away from the origin as $t \\to \\infty,$ so the origin is an unstable node.\n\nThe phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/b3/b32b5666c0138f5c17ff1622cef9bca6fb019375f6748b6422c11a547cfad282.png)" 13194139535861 17592186045417 false] [17592186168005 1064 "Let's sketch the phase portrait of the following linear system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting our system in matrix form, we have\n![](images/f5/f58fb57caaba8c5f6a110c72565c24803ac233f41b8eb2db0c5221bcf12d7fe2.svg)\nwhere ![](images/65/65298928a44147868c82da753a731e35c3aa0ad5e4b3400819d9d9f16f546a34.svg)\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=2$ with the corresponding eigenvectors $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and ![](images/69/694a89326f61bbbba7e91db5dcb607a74f99881ce16059450dbe1bbac7ee797e.svg)\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n![](images/ab/abd4dbe3156abbcd645e670fb893f93cc8765cba4b1da9bde11075e211f1eeaa.svg)\n\nNotice that for each vector ![](images/9d/9df6cb20bf3163f53850730242138ec62412ccc8af23098130d71228cac4b82a.svg) we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/71/7166c456c7912bf31d4ff2d98f4e4206537f8d01aca086fdba3c15b394db4b81.svg)\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector ![](images/23/23074d10911b113811cc92bdc88a61a6758dbd8772c21425159c5e95d30b1075.svg)\n\nSince $e^{2t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions move away from the origin as $t \\to \\infty,$ so the origin is an unstable node.\n\nThe phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/b3/b32b5666c0138f5c17ff1622cef9bca6fb019375f6748b6422c11a547cfad282.png)" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186168006 1064 "Consider the following system of linear differential equations: \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue of the system's matrix $A$ is ![](images/23/23616d197363a3fd8ad4689a0b1e547c7b1044e0929cfd96add65a26e940731f.svg) The vector ![](images/3e/3e434cc0b5ca5d288695abb58500e8f4ca6be0c0cf2ecb5200d03f5fbcf522c2.svg) is such that $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$ \n\nLet's sketch the phase portrait of the system.\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=1$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $\\mathbf{v},$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}  + c_2 (\\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t} \\, ),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$\n\nIn our case, \n![](images/0f/0f7c56980abff1ad84ca69aa98730bca2988cf5430f181f69d191f1e3c28eff0.svg) \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/c9/c9d65d3792c7cced40b86834fbd66769daa714ed58ffcde025cb956763c2c670.svg)\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since $e^{t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions move toward infinity in the phase plane as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/98/98fc60d566b520f3d943e42ee8f4e07aa0bdd33b53fbb04981c2e8f4001210fd.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n![](images/61/6170eec4537e26afef2a794426b4f5231b0f4366c21b8860c332f1ec7573b96a.svg)\nWhen $c_2=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=t{e}^{t} \\\\ y &={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $1.2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-0.30$ | $0$ | $3.98$ |\n| $y$ | $0.61$ | $1$ | $3.32$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/4f/4fbc1650377d580d7129701d745dc8cad89d2d1d061b8f6dca8127f0ce062dc8.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/49/499391b5bc015bd96bbe85d72e3937e87a5e89081951aaad0a752582cb87f15f.png)" 13194139535861 17592186045417 false] [17592186168006 1064 "Consider the following system of linear differential equations: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue of the system's matrix $A$ is ![](images/23/23616d197363a3fd8ad4689a0b1e547c7b1044e0929cfd96add65a26e940731f.svg) The vector ![](images/3e/3e434cc0b5ca5d288695abb58500e8f4ca6be0c0cf2ecb5200d03f5fbcf522c2.svg) is such that $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$ \n\nLet's sketch the phase portrait of the system.\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=1$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $\\mathbf{v},$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}  + c_2 (\\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t} \\, ),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$\n\nIn our case, \n![](images/0f/0f7c56980abff1ad84ca69aa98730bca2988cf5430f181f69d191f1e3c28eff0.svg) \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/c9/c9d65d3792c7cced40b86834fbd66769daa714ed58ffcde025cb956763c2c670.svg)\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since $e^{t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions move toward infinity in the phase plane as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/98/98fc60d566b520f3d943e42ee8f4e07aa0bdd33b53fbb04981c2e8f4001210fd.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n![](images/61/6170eec4537e26afef2a794426b4f5231b0f4366c21b8860c332f1ec7573b96a.svg)\nWhen $c_2=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t{e}^{t} \\\\ y={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $1.2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-0.30$ | $0$ | $3.98$ |\n| $y$ | $0.61$ | $1$ | $3.32$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/4f/4fbc1650377d580d7129701d745dc8cad89d2d1d061b8f6dca8127f0ce062dc8.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/49/499391b5bc015bd96bbe85d72e3937e87a5e89081951aaad0a752582cb87f15f.png)" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186168010 1064 "Consider the system of linear differential equations below.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=\\frac{1}{2}x(t)-4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=4x(t)+\\frac{1}{2}y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_{1,2} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\pm4\\text{i},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_{1,2} = [\\pm \\text{i}, 1]^T,$ respectively. Given that the general solution of the system is\n![](images/d1/d13abb97b171049a17544fcb312b71d2da3d9294788c1aebe7aa969e99b7819c.svg)\nwe are interested in sketching the phase portrait of the system of differential equations given above.\n\nIf we set $c_1 = 1$ and $c_2 = 0$ in the general solution of the system, we obtain the particular solution:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &={e}^{t/2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ y &={e}^{t/2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}{16}$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $0.78$ | $0$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $0.78$ | $1.22$ |\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](images/4f/4f9da4b66977b98b983477d22abb6e00135ea0e1bfa9f785cd0a62d674a7b782.png)\n\nNotice that $|\\sin(4t)| \\leq 1$ and $|\\cos(4t)| \\leq 1,$ but $e^{t/2} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty.$ Thus, the obtained curve is unbounded. So, we have a spiral that is traversed counterclockwise.\n\nAgain, since $e^{t/2} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ the spiral is traversed toward infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/62/62380812d6fbe4e28531a455814296a4969a0744fb28a94a5e2d7c588ec5cfc4.png)" 13194139535861 17592186045417 false] [17592186168010 1064 "Consider the system of linear differential equations below.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}x(t)-4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4x(t)+\\frac{1}{2}y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_{1,2} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\pm4\\text{i},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_{1,2} = [\\pm \\text{i}, 1]^T,$ respectively. Given that the general solution of the system is\n![](images/d1/d13abb97b171049a17544fcb312b71d2da3d9294788c1aebe7aa969e99b7819c.svg)\nwe are interested in sketching the phase portrait of the system of differential equations given above.\n\nIf we set $c_1 = 1$ and $c_2 = 0$ in the general solution of the system, we obtain the particular solution:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x={e}^{t/2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ y={e}^{t/2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}{16}$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $0.78$ | $0$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $0.78$ | $1.22$ |\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](images/4f/4f9da4b66977b98b983477d22abb6e00135ea0e1bfa9f785cd0a62d674a7b782.png)\n\nNotice that $|\\sin(4t)| \\leq 1$ and $|\\cos(4t)| \\leq 1,$ but $e^{t/2} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty.$ Thus, the obtained curve is unbounded. So, we have a spiral that is traversed counterclockwise.\n\nAgain, since $e^{t/2} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ the spiral is traversed toward infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/62/62380812d6fbe4e28531a455814296a4969a0744fb28a94a5e2d7c588ec5cfc4.png)" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186173524 1075 #edb/ref 17592186173525 13194139535861 17592186045417 false] [17592186173524 1075 #edb/ref 17592186173526 13194139535861 17592186045417 false] [17592186173524 1075 #edb/ref 17592186173527 13194139535861 17592186045417 false] [17592186173524 1075 #edb/ref 17592186173528 13194139535861 17592186045417 false] [17592186173524 1075 #edb/ref 17592186173529 13194139535861 17592186045417 false] [17592186173524 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533358 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186173524 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533530 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186173524 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533695 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186173524 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533746 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186173524 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533751 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186173524 1078 #edb/ref 17592186533746 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186175996 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534050 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186175996 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534089 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186176211 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186176211 1071 "$$\n{∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A,\\,D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,2\\le y\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of $z=xy$ for $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $2\\le y\\le 3.$ What is the missing expression?" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186176211 1071 "$∬_(D)AAAAAAAAdA, D = {(x, y): 1 \\le x \\le 2, 2 \\le y \\le 3}$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of $z = xy$ for $1 \\le x \\le 2$ and $2 \\le y \\le 3$. What is the missing expression?" 13194139535861 17592186045417 false] [17592186176211 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=xy,\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,2\\le y\\le 3.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)=y, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy)=x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(y)^{2}+(x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186176214 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $y=f(x,z)$ with $(x,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+z,\\,(x,z)\\in D,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le z\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/be/be5dc3bf3ed4b6b7bee06d583b69e40a3922edede1e61a6f0b68d2ce0767f92a.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂y}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+z)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+z)=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{2}+(1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{2+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}\\sqrt{2+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot z\\,{|}_{z=0}^{z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\sqrt{2+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=2+{cos}^{2}⁡x,$ we get $\\text{d}u=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ u & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\sqrt{2+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-{\\int }_{3}^{2}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}({3}^{3/2}-{2}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(3\\sqrt{3}-2\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186176214 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $y=f(x,z)$ with $(x,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+z,\\,(x,z)\\in D,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le z\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/be/be5dc3bf3ed4b6b7bee06d583b69e40a3922edede1e61a6f0b68d2ce0767f92a.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂y}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+z)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+z)=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{2}+(1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{2+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}\\sqrt{2+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot z\\,{|}_{z=0}^{z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\sqrt{2+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=2+{cos}^{2}⁡x,$ we get $\\text{d}u=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ u & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\sqrt{2+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-{\\int }_{3}^{2}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}({3}^{3/2}-{2}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(3\\sqrt{3}-2\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 false] [17592186176243 1068 #edb/ref 17592186173716 13194139535861 17592186045417 false] [17592186176243 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz={x}^{2}+{y}^{2},\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le x\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/0b/0b6de7829e702377d1b20bb1b297d3d28e6fb8bae9672b0137d92b4d53a96c83.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(2x)^{2}+(2y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186176243 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz={x}^{2}+{y}^{2},\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le x},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/0b/0b6de7829e702377d1b20bb1b297d3d28e6fb8bae9672b0137d92b4d53a96c83.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}