50037 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533970 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050866 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533970 1089 #edb/ref 17592186533971 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533970 1090 #uuid "79110b9e-fd73-5efb-8ce0-acef8691262c" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533971 1084 #edb/ref 17592186534100 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533971 1084 #edb/ref 17592186534101 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533971 1084 #edb/ref 17592186534114 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533971 1084 #edb/ref 17592186534125 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533971 1084 #edb/ref 17592186534135 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533971 1084 #edb/ref 17592186534137 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533971 1084 #edb/ref 17592186534138 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533971 1084 #edb/ref 17592186534150 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533971 1085 "Let $f(x)$ be a continuously differentiable function defined for all real $x.$ Values for $f$ and some of its derivatives at particular points are shown below.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n| **$x$** | **$f(x)$** | **${f}^{′}(x)$** | **${f}^{″}(x)$** | **${f}^{‴}(x)$** | **${f}^{(4)}(x)$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $9$ | $-1$ | $-5$ | $-4$ | $-2$ |\n| $3$ | $6$ | $-5$ | $-6$ | $-6$ | $-3$ |\n| $4$ | $-5$ | $-7$ | $∗$ | $-13$ | $-6$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nIn the interval $x\\in [2,4],$ the function $f$ and its derivatives are all decreasing." 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533971 1086 #uuid "8fc7ff53-1fcb-5909-be7f-1625560888f4" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533971 1087 #edb/ref 17592186534138 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533972 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533968 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533972 1069 "q-355398" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533972 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533972 1071 "Let $c(n)$ be a cost for option $n,$ where $n\\in \\{3,5,7,9\\}.$ We want to write the optimal option $\\overset{^}{n}$ as \"the value of $n$ that makes $c(n)$ as small as possible.\"\n\nWhich of the following correctly defines $\\overset{^}{n}?$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533972 1072 #uuid "00fdbc42-937e-5a35-be79-3ffa566504b0" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533972 1074 "We want $\\overset{^}{n}$ to be an *input value* $n$ where the cost $c(n)$ is as small as possible. \n\nThat is exactly what $\\operatorname{arg}⁡min$ returns.\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{n}=\\operatorname{arg}⁡{min}_{n\\in \\{3,5,7,9\\}}c(n).\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533973 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533860 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533973 1069 "q-159886" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533973 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533973 1071 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4},{X}_{5}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼\\text{Exp}(\\theta )$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ Find the likelihood function of the sample $0.6,\\,1.8,\\,0.7,\\,2.9,\\,1.5.$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533973 1072 #uuid "0422034f-fdb1-53c7-b35d-c57baa06d2e8" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533973 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=5$ independent exponential random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x},\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\theta {e}^{-\\theta {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\,{e}^{-\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\,{e}^{-S\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$ \n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =0.6+1.8+0.7+2.9+1.5 \\\\  & =7.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=5.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\theta }^{5}\\,{e}^{-7.5\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533974 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533901 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533974 1069 "q-159952" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533974 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533974 1071 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{6}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼N(\\theta ,1),$  where $\\theta $ is an unknown mean. For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{6},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{6}{x}_{i}=5.2,\\,{\\sum }_{i=1}^{6}{x}_{i}^{2}=22.6.\n$$\n\nGiven that the likelihood function of the sample is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=\\frac{1}{8{\\pi }^{3}}\\,{e}^{\\,g(\\theta )},\n$$\n\nwhat is the expression for $g(\\theta )?$\n\n*Hint: The probability density function for a normal random variable with mean $\\theta $ and variance $1$ is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533974 1072 #uuid "0731a97b-5085-5ebd-87e8-36cd1dc34ba4" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533974 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=6$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta $ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-\\sum ({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(\\sum {x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(C-2S\\theta +n{\\theta }^{2})/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $C={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}.$\n\nWe are told that $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=5.2,$ $C={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}=22.6,$ and $n=6.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =(2\\pi )^{-6/2}\\,{e}^{-(22.6-2(5.2)\\theta +6{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8{\\pi }^{3}}\\,{e}^{\\,-11.3\\,+\\,5.2\\theta \\,-\\,3{\\theta }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(\\theta )=-11.3+5.2\\theta -3{\\theta }^{2}.\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533975 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533940 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533975 1069 "q-355401" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533975 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533975 1071 "Let $w(y)$ be a weight for node $y,$ where $y\\in \\{-7,-5,-3,-1\\}.$ We want to write the selected node $\\overset{^}{y}$ as \"the value of $y$ that makes $w(y)$ as large as possible.\"\n\nWhich of the following correctly defines $\\overset{^}{y}?$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533975 1072 #uuid "0e94aa36-f8ac-5d68-8a85-88c135136bde" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533975 1074 "We want $\\overset{^}{y}$ to be an *input value* $y$ where the weight $w(y)$ is as large as possible. \n\nThat is exactly what $\\operatorname{arg}⁡max$ returns.\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{y}=\\operatorname{arg}⁡{max}_{y\\in \\{-7,-5,-3,-1\\}}w(y).\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533976 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533799 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533976 1069 "q-124191" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533976 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533976 1071 "${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4n(n+1)}=$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533976 1072 #uuid "1074656f-8ae1-5d9c-a611-c60b94fb1e33" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533976 1074 "First, note that the series can be written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{4}{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n(n+1)}.\n$$\n\nUsing partial fractions we can write the series as\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{4}{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }(\\frac{1}{n}-\\frac{1}{n+1}).\n$$\n\nComputing the first few partial sums, starting with $n=2,$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}[(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{3})] \\\\ {s}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{4}[(\\frac{1}{2}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}}-\\frac{1}{4})] \\\\ {s}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{4}[(\\frac{1}{2}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}}-\\frac{1}{5})] \\\\ {s}_{5} & =\\frac{1}{4}[(\\frac{1}{2}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}}-\\frac{1}{6})].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that for each partial sum, we always end up canceling all terms with the exception of the first and last terms. Therefore, the $N$th partial sum is\n\n$$\n{s}_{N}=\\frac{1}{4}[\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{N+1}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we can determine the sum of the series by taking the limit of ${s}_{N}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4n(n+1)} & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }{s}_{N} \\\\  & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{4}[\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{N+1}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\frac{1}{2}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533977 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533833 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533977 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533848 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533977 1069 "q-333626" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533977 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533977 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above, where the system's matrix has only one eigenvalue. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.\n\n$\\,$ The equilibrium point (origin) is a \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\nnode." 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533977 1072 #uuid "130e77e3-e68d-5bfc-a672-f433d0d78798" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533977 1074 "The matrix of our system is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is $\\lambda =4$ and it's positive, we have a $\\text{unstable}$ node.\n\n- Since $A$ is not diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there is only one linearly independent eigenvector. So, we have a $\\text{degenerate}$ node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f6/f67469d62d6a0eb61397927924e55868437ad09f405c3b2a9d73567af00c4620.png)" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533978 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533922 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533978 1069 "q-555460" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533978 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533978 1071 "Between which two consecutive tenths does $\\sqrt{30}$ lie?" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533978 1072 #uuid "14353486-3eaf-5d78-ba59-74f924378a4f" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533978 1074 "To find the two consecutive tenths between which $\\sqrt{30}$ lies, we need to find two numbers with one decimal place whose squares bracket $30$.\n\nFirst, let's establish between which two consecutive integers $\\sqrt{30}$ lies.\nWe know that ${5}^{2}=25$ and ${6}^{2}=36$.\nSince $25<30<36$, we know that $5<\\sqrt{30}<6$.\n\nNext, we can test decimals between $5$ and $6$ to narrow down the interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5.3}^{2} & =28.09 \\\\ {5.4}^{2} & =29.16 \\\\ {5.5}^{2} & =30.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $29.16<30<30.25$, we can write:\n\n$$\n{5.4}^{2}<30<{5.5}^{2}\n$$\n\nTaking the square root of each part of the inequality gives:\n\n$$\n5.4<\\sqrt{30}<5.5\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\sqrt{30}$ lies between $5.4$ and $5.5$." 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533979 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533881 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533979 1069 "q-53562" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533979 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533979 1071 "${\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{n(n+1)}=$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533979 1072 #uuid "14bccc9f-2da5-5aa2-aa0a-d74284957688" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533979 1074 "First, note that the series can be written as\n\n$$\n6{\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n(n+1)}.\n$$\n\nUsing partial fractions, we can write the series as\n\n$$\n6{\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }(\\frac{1}{n}-\\frac{1}{n+1}).\n$$\n\nComputing the first few partial sums, starting with $n=3,$ gives\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{3} & =6[(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{4})] \\\\ {s}_{4} & =6[(\\frac{1}{3}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}}-\\frac{1}{5})] \\\\ {s}_{5} & =6[(\\frac{1}{3}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}}-\\frac{1}{6})] \\\\ {s}_{6} & =6[(\\frac{1}{3}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}}-\\frac{1}{7})].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in each partial sum, we always end up canceling all terms except for the first and last terms. Therefore, the $N$th partial sum is\n\n$$\n{s}_{N}=6[\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{N+1}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we can determine the sum of the series by taking the limit of ${s}_{N}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{n(n+1)} & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }{s}_{N} \\\\  & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }6[\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{N+1}] \\\\  & =6[\\frac{1}{3}-0] \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533980 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533961 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533980 1069 "q-186161" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533980 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533980 1071 "Evaluate the following:\n\n$\\sqrt[3]{-1,000}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533980 1072 #uuid "15f99678-56db-56d3-a971-804057202f02" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533980 1074 "First, notice that the number $-1,000$ is a perfect cube.\n\nSo, to determine the value of $\\sqrt[3]{-1,000},$ we express $-1,000$ as a perfect cube:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{-1,000} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{(-10)\\cdot (-10)\\cdot (-10)} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{(-10)^{3}} & = \\\\ -10 & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533981 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533919 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533981 1069 "q-124095" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533981 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533981 1071 "${\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{3n(n+1)}=$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533981 1072 #uuid "183b979f-8612-5577-8e86-5be342266cb9" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533981 1074 "First, note that the series can be written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{3}{\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n(n+1)}.\n$$\n\nUsing partial fractions we can write the series as\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{3}{\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }(\\frac{1}{n}-\\frac{1}{n+1}).\n$$\n\nComputing the first few partial sums, starting with $n=5,$ gives\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{5} & =\\frac{4}{3}[(\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{6})] \\\\ {s}_{6} & =\\frac{4}{3}[(\\frac{1}{5}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}}-\\frac{1}{7})] \\\\ {s}_{7} & =\\frac{4}{3}[(\\frac{1}{5}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}}-\\frac{1}{8})] \\\\ {s}_{8} & =\\frac{4}{3}[(\\frac{1}{5}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{8}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{8}}-\\frac{1}{9})].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that for each partial sum, we always end up canceling all terms with the exception of the first and last terms. Therefore, the $N$th partial sum is\n\n$$\n{s}_{N}=\\frac{4}{3}[\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{N+1}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we can determine the sum of the series by taking the limit of ${s}_{N}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{3n(n+1)} & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }{s}_{N} \\\\  & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }\\frac{4}{3}[\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{N+1}] \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}[\\frac{1}{5}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533982 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533953 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533982 1069 "q-127237" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533982 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533982 1071 "Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ found in part 1 to estimate the value of $f(4.1).$\n\n$f(4.1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533982 1072 #uuid "18880848-70e4-5a06-adb0-54fd08f27bb7" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533982 1074 "Our tangent line approximation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =-7x+23.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(4.1)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=4.1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4.1) & ≈L(4.1) \\\\  & =-7(4.1)+23 \\\\  & =-5.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533983 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533796 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533983 1069 "q-159920" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533983 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533983 1071 "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}$ be I.I.D. random variables with probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=(\\theta +1){x}^{-\\theta -2},\\,x\\ge 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter. Find the likelihood function of the sample $1.5,\\,1.8,\\,2.0.$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533983 1072 #uuid "1b3b7675-fd44-5cc4-931a-4915684e1c8d" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533983 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=3$ independent random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=(\\theta +1){x}^{-\\theta -2},\\,x\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}((\\theta +1){x}_{i}^{-\\theta -2}) \\\\  & =(\\theta +1)^{n}\\cdot {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{-\\theta -2} \\\\  & =(\\theta +1)^{n}({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})^{-\\theta -2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =1.5\\cdot 1.8\\cdot 2=5.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=3.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=(\\theta +1)^{3}(5.4)^{-\\theta -2}.\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533984 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533773 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533984 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533806 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533984 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533896 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533984 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533964 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533984 1069 "q-336094" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533984 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533984 1071 "Consider the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant $2\\times 2$ matrix. Let $Φ(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$ is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ be the columns of $Φ(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are linearly independent for all $t$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $det(Φ(t))\\ne 0$ for all $t$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L1}$ introduces the notation for the {{field-1}} of {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from line {{field-4}} by the properties of determinants." 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533984 1072 #uuid "20281150-3bf1-5302-a5be-765695a3b8d8" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533984 1074 "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first interpret line $\\text{L1}.$ Line $\\text{L1}$ introduces the notation for the $\\text{columns}$ of $Φ,$ namely the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ These functions are the fundamental solutions of the system.\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ Since $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis of the solution space for our system. Thus, $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{definition of a fundamental matrix}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}.$ Recall that a matrix has linearly independent columns if and only if its determinant is nonzero. Since ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are linearly independent for any particular $t,$\n\n$$\ndet(Φ(t))\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L3}$ follows from line $\\text{L2}$ by the properties of determinants." 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533985 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533842 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533985 1069 "q-10208" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533985 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533985 1071 "![](images/b0/b0d11e89592cad64ebbeb72def6ca0a6c5880a9a54f82b394d7857e142c05edf.png)\n\nFind the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx={t}^{3}-4t,\\,y={t}^{2}-1\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $t\\in [0,1].$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533985 1072 #uuid "26258e04-30b1-50d5-abea-f32adfbd6868" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533985 1074 "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=y(t),\\,{t}_{1}\\le t\\le {t}_{2}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-4t) \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}-1)(3{t}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{t}^{4}-7{t}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a positive number since $y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\ge 0$ for all $t\\in [0,1].$\n\nTherefore, the area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{t}^{4}-7{t}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}t-7{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t+4{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3\\cdot {\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}\\,|}_{0}^{1}-7\\cdot {\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}\\,|}_{0}^{1}+4\\cdot t\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{5}-0)-7(\\frac{1}{3}-0)+4(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}-\\frac{7}{3}+4 \\\\  & =\\frac{34}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533986 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533879 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533986 1069 "q-269383" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533986 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533986 1071 "Consider the function $L:{R}^{3\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=3({y}^{T}Ax)$ where \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533986 1072 #uuid "26793c1a-40f5-5bd2-9877-7d31b77eb2ea" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533986 1074 "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWriting out $L$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\nL={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}3{a}_{ij}{y}_{i}{x}_{j},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}=3{y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${∇}_{A}L=3(y\\cdot {x}^{T}).$\n\nEvaluating at the given values $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=3([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 5\\end{aligned}])=3([\\begin{aligned}2 & 10 \\\\ -1 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 15\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 30 \\\\ -3 & -15 \\\\ 9 & 45\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533987 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533810 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533987 1069 "q-21261" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533987 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533987 1071 "![](images/a8/a886f775367f244f78087935cb717b988d85f1714b2cae7ce3e4ba2525ef178d.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]?$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533987 1072 #uuid "28dbcab1-e3b5-5a8b-9a9c-02483c0b7b4f" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533987 1074 "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=y(t),\\,{t}_{1}\\le t\\le {t}_{2}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a positive number since $y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\ge 0$ for all $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nTherefore, the area is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533988 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533830 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533988 1069 "q-178541" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533988 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533988 1071 "The additive inverse of $30$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533988 1072 #uuid "2c77dd09-2d15-50f1-838b-9b9dd6ed6be5" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533988 1074 "To find the additive inverse of the number $30$, we change its sign by placing a minus symbol $(-)$ in front of it. So, we obtain \n\n$$\n-\\,(30)=-30.\n$$\n\nTo check our work, we simply add the inverse to the original number. If we're correct, we should get zero.\n\n$$\n30+(-30)=30-30=0\\,✓\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533989 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533958 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533989 1069 "q-111795" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533989 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533989 1071 "Which value of $q$ gives precisely one solution to the equation ${x}^{2}+q+3=7?$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533989 1072 #uuid "306bf2f9-520c-5fa5-bb34-4cf2b1fae3a0" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533989 1074 "First, we solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+q+3 & =7 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =7-q-3 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4-q \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{4-q} \\\\ |x| & =\\sqrt{4-q}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation has only one solution if the right-hand side is equal to zero, and the right-hand side is zero only if the expression underneath the radical is zero. So, we solve for the value of $q$ that makes the expression under the radical equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-q & =0 \\\\ q & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $q=4$ will give only one solution to the equation." 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533990 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533960 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533990 1069 "q-111226" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533990 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533990 1071 "Classify the polynomial $-\\frac{2t}{5}$ according to its degree and the number of terms." 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533990 1072 #uuid "320d3413-9845-54f4-9740-6e67695ad26e" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533990 1074 "The polynomial $-\\frac{2t}{5}$ has only one term, so it is a monomial.\n\nAlso, its degree is $1,$ so it is a linear.\n\nTherefore, the polynomial is a linear monomial." 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533991 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533795 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533991 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533829 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533991 1069 "q-187260" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533991 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533991 1071 "Complete the ratio table below.\n\n| $2$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $6$ |\n| {{field-2}} | $18$ |" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533991 1072 #uuid "35308452-763c-5cea-932c-69c3b89da098" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533991 1074 "We start with the ratio $4:6$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{6}\n$$\n\nSo the numerator represents the left-hand entry ($4$), and the denominator represents the right-hand entry ($6$).\n\nWe can generate equivalent ratios by finding equivalent fractions:\n\n- To create a numerator of $2,$ we divide the numerator and denominator of our fraction by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4÷2}{6÷2}=\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To create a denominator of $18,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator of the previous fraction by $6:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\times 6}{3\\times 6} & =\\frac{12}{18}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we fill in the ratio table:\n\n| $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $6$ |\n| $12$ | $18$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533992 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533846 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533992 1069 "q-265777" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533992 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533992 1071 "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(X)=(det(X))^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is the gradient of $f$ with respect to $X$ at $X=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533992 1072 #uuid "35662c2c-d0a2-5e1c-b319-cae7bd255ca0" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533992 1074 "The gradient of a function $f:{R}^{m\\times n}\\to R$ with respect to a matrix $X\\in {R}^{m\\times n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{mn}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $f(X)$ where $X=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11} & {x}_{12} \\\\ {x}_{21} & {x}_{22}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{2\\times 2}$, we get\n\n$$\nf(X)=(det(X))^{2}=({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,{∇}_{X}f(X) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{11}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2} & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{12}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{21}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2} & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{22}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot {x}_{22} & 2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot (-{x}_{21}) \\\\ 2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot (-{x}_{12}) & 2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot {x}_{11}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{22} & -{x}_{21} \\\\ -{x}_{12} & {x}_{11}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2det(X)[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{22} & -{x}_{21} \\\\ -{x}_{12} & {x}_{11}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $X=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=2(4\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}14 & -42 \\\\ 14 & 56\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533993 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533898 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533993 1069 "q-265773" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533993 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533993 1071 "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(X)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(det(X)).\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the gradient of $f$ with respect to $X$ at $X=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533993 1072 #uuid "378ecb1a-5423-57cf-9a92-70fe30005016" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533993 1074 "The gradient of a function $f:{R}^{m\\times n}\\to R$ with respect to a matrix $X\\in {R}^{m\\times n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{mn}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $f(X)$ where $X=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11} & {x}_{12} \\\\ {x}_{21} & {x}_{22}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{2\\times 2}$, we get\n\n$$\nf(X)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(det(X))=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}).\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,{∇}_{X}f(X) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{11}}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{12}}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{21}}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{22}}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}))\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-{x}_{22}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} & \\frac{{x}_{21}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} \\\\ \\frac{{x}_{12}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} & \\frac{-{x}_{11}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $X=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{-4-(-3)} & \\frac{1}{-4-(-3)} \\\\ \\frac{-3}{-4-(-3)} & -\\frac{(-1)}{-4-(-3)}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{-1} & \\frac{1}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{-3}{-1} & -\\frac{(-1)}{-1}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533994 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533910 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533994 1069 "q-138510" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533994 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533994 1071 "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 12\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the second principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $13$ and $3.$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533994 1072 #uuid "39ac48e2-8478-529d-91f9-60e771cc4eb9" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533994 1074 "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are ${\\lambda }_{1}=13$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 12\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-3I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533995 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533942 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533995 1069 "q-352359" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533995 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533995 1071 "Evaluate the series\n${\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{(n+3)(n+4)}.$\nDo not round the answer.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $\\frac{1}{(n+3)(n+4)}=\\frac{1}{n+3}-\\frac{1}{n+4}.$*\n\n${\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{(n+3)(n+4)}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533995 1072 #uuid "3aca4a3b-d87a-5bf6-923c-3d648e553340" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533995 1074 "First, note that the series can be written as\n\n$$\n2{\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(n+3)(n+4)}\n$$\n\nWe are also given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(n+3)(n+4)}=\\frac{1}{n+3}-\\frac{1}{n+4}.\n$$\n\nSo, our series can be written as\n\n$$\n2{\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }(\\frac{1}{n+3}-\\frac{1}{n+4}).\n$$\n\nLet's now compute the first few partial sums:\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{3} & =2[(\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{1}{7})] \\\\ {s}_{4} & =2[(\\frac{1}{6}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}}-\\frac{1}{8})] \\\\ {s}_{5} & =2[(\\frac{1}{6}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{8}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{8}}-\\frac{1}{9})] \\\\ {s}_{6} & =2[(\\frac{1}{6}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{8}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{8}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{9}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{9}}-\\frac{1}{10})]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in each partial sum, we always end up canceling all terms except for the first and last terms. Therefore, the $N$th partial sum is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{N} & =2(\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{1}{N+4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can determine the sum of the series by taking the limit of ${s}_{N}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{(n+3)(n+4)} & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }{s}_{N} \\\\  & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }2(\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{1}{N+4}) \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{6}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533996 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533797 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533996 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533822 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533996 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533862 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533996 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533937 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533996 1069 "q-355223" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533996 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533996 1071 "| $t$ | $g(t)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $-4$ | $-2$ |\n| $-2$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $-1$ |\n| $3$ | $5$ |\n\nThe table above gives values of a function $g$ on the set $T=\\{-4,-2,2,3\\}.$ Compute the following:\n\n${max}_{t\\in T}g(t)=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\operatorname{arg}⁡{max}_{t\\in T}g(t)=$\n{{field-2}}\n\n${min}_{t\\in T}g(t)=$\n{{field-3}}\n\n$\\operatorname{arg}⁡{min}_{t\\in T}g(t)=$\n{{field-4}}" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533996 1072 #uuid "3ad87e41-53b3-5b37-8281-b66e69b1893c" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533996 1074 "Recall that \n\n- ${max}_{x\\in S}f(x)$ is the maximum output value, while\n\n- $\\operatorname{arg}⁡{max}_{x\\in S}f(x)$ is an input in $S$ where that maximum output occurs.\n\nOn the other hand,\n\n- ${min}_{x\\in S}f(x)$ is the minimum output value, while\n\n- $\\operatorname{arg}⁡{min}_{x\\in S}f(x)$ is an input in $S$ where that minimum output occurs.\n\nThe output values are $-2,1,-1,$ and $5.$ The maximum output is $5$ (at $t=3$), and the minimum output is $-2$ (at $t=-4$). \n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{max}_{t\\in T}g(t) & =5 \\\\ \\operatorname{arg}⁡{max}_{t\\in T}g(t) & =3 \\\\ {min}_{t\\in T}g(t) & =-2 \\\\ \\operatorname{arg}⁡{min}_{t\\in T}g(t) & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533997 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533911 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533997 1069 "q-555923" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533997 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533997 1071 "Estimate the value of $\\sqrt{72},$ rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\n$\\sqrt{72}≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533997 1072 #uuid "3e4b5639-d116-5036-9ab2-4b00a6b27dd4" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533997 1074 "To estimate $\\sqrt{72},$ we first find the two perfect squares closest to $72.$\n\nThe closest perfect squares are $64={8}^{2}$ and $81={9}^{2}.$\n\nSince $64<72<81,$ we know that the square root of $72$ lies between $8$ and $9.$\n\n![](images/e9/e931f5e15b35adb850966e40e4ad759c233d9419771998efba5dc91d9ab95bc1.png)\n\nSince $72$ lies between $64$ and $81,$ we expect $\\sqrt{72}$ to lie between $8$ and $9.$\n\nLet's test decimals near the middle by squaring them:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{8.4}^{2} & =70.56 \\\\ {8.5}^{2} & =72.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that $72$ lies between $70.56$ and $72.25,$ so $8.4<\\sqrt{72}<8.5.$\n\nNext, we determine which value is closer to $72:$\n\n- The distance from $72$ to ${8.4}^{2}$ is $72-70.56=1.44.$\n\n- The distance from $72$ to ${8.5}^{2}$ is $72.25-72=0.25.$\n\nSince $72$ is much closer to $72.25,$ $\\sqrt{72}$ is closer to $8.5.$\n\nTherefore, rounded to $1$ decimal place, $\\sqrt{72}≈8.5.$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533998 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533906 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533998 1069 "q-346722" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533998 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533998 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{9-2x}{x(x-3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533998 1072 #uuid "4254f874-a296-5085-b2f1-6690320d6f45" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533998 1074 "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{9-2x}{x(x-3)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{B}{x-3}+\\frac{C}{(x-3)^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $x(x-3)^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n9-2x=A(x-3)^{2}+Bx(x-3)+Cx.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0,$ we can eliminate $B$ and $C$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9-2(0) & =A(0-3)^{2}+B(0)(0-3)+C(0) \\\\ 9 & =9A \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=3,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9-2(3) & =A(3-3)^{2}+B(3)(3-3)+C(3) \\\\ 3 & =3C \\\\ C & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=1$ and substituting the known values of $A$ and $C,$ we can solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9-2(1) & =A(1-3)^{2}+B(1)(1-3)+C(1) \\\\ 7 & =4A-2B+C \\\\ 7 & =4(1)-2B+1 \\\\ B & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{9-2x}{x(x-3)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{1}{x-3}+\\frac{1}{(x-3)^{2}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{9-2x}{x(x-3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{1}{x-3}+\\frac{1}{(x-3)^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{(x-3)^{2}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|\\,{|}_{1}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|\\,{|}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{x-3}\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-(-1+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533999 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533834 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533999 1069 "q-138506" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533999 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533999 1071 "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the first principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $1$ and $6.$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533999 1072 #uuid "43345541-f657-59ff-989d-45d4cfc772b4" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186533999 1074 "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are ${\\lambda }_{1}=6$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=6:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-6I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-6I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534000 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533886 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534000 1069 "q-269374" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534000 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534000 1071 "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)={y}^{T}Ax$ where \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534000 1072 #uuid "46d18a08-f2dc-5cb8-81b8-c0b6c16aa5ec" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534000 1074 "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWriting out $L$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\nL={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{a}_{ij}{y}_{i}{x}_{j},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${∇}_{A}L=y\\cdot {x}^{T}.$\n\nEvaluating at the given values $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534001 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533849 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534001 1069 "q-222408" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534001 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534001 1071 "The solution to $-9(8a+2)^{2}=0$ is $a=${{field-1}}." 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534001 1072 #uuid "49274b7d-2639-522a-95c1-022f42cefd65" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534001 1074 "We can solve this equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9(8a+2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ (8a+2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{(8a+2)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{0} \\\\ |8a+2| & =0 \\\\ 8a+2 & =0 \\\\ 8a & =-2 \\\\ a & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534002 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533902 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534002 1069 "q-129344" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534002 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534002 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x-y}-4}{16-2x+y}=$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534002 1072 #uuid "4ad2c176-6641-542e-87e9-2e557825f205" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534002 1074 "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x-y}-4}{16-2x+y} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2(7)-(-2)}-4}{16-2(7)+(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-4}{16-14-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x-y}-4}{16-2x+y} & ={lim}_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{2x-y}-4)(\\sqrt{2x-y}+4)}{(16-2x+y)(\\sqrt{2x-y}+4)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{2x-y-16}{(16-2x+y)(\\sqrt{2x-y}+4)} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{16-2x+y}{(16-2x+y)(\\sqrt{2x-y}+4)} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{\\cancel{16-2x+y}}{\\cancel{(16-2x+y)}(\\sqrt{2x-y}+4)} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-y}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{lim}_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-y}+4} & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2(7)-(-2)}+4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{16}+4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4+4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-\\frac{1}{8}.$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534003 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533838 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534003 1069 "q-265980" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534003 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534003 1071 "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(X)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{x}_{ij}^{2}\n$$\n\n \nwhere ${x}_{ij}$ are the corresponding matrix's entries. What is the gradient of $f$ with respect to $X$ at $X=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534003 1072 #uuid "4c895033-c45a-5568-ba41-7003b1ce0b16" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534003 1074 "The gradient of a function $f:{R}^{m\\times n}\\to R$ with respect to a matrix $X\\in {R}^{m\\times n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{mn}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $f(X)$ where $X=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11} & {x}_{12} \\\\ {x}_{21} & {x}_{22}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{2\\times 2}$, we get\n\n$$\nf(X)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{x}_{ij}^{2}=({x}_{11}^{2}+{x}_{12}^{2}+{x}_{21}^{2}+{x}_{22}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,{∇}_{X}f(X) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{11}}({x}_{11}^{2}+{x}_{12}^{2}+{x}_{21}^{2}+{x}_{22}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{12}}({x}_{11}^{2}+{x}_{12}^{2}+{x}_{21}^{2}+{x}_{22}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{21}}({x}_{11}^{2}+{x}_{12}^{2}+{x}_{21}^{2}+{x}_{22}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{22}}({x}_{11}^{2}+{x}_{12}^{2}+{x}_{21}^{2}+{x}_{22}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{11} & 2{x}_{12} \\\\ 2{x}_{21} & 2{x}_{22}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11} & {x}_{12} \\\\ {x}_{21} & {x}_{22}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2X.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $X=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot (1) & 2\\cdot (5) \\\\ 2\\cdot (-3) & 2\\cdot (4)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 10 \\\\ -6 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534004 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533769 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534004 1069 "q-111848" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534004 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534004 1071 "Given that  $9-24r+16{r}^{2}=0$, find $r.$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534004 1072 #uuid "511786af-e85b-5860-b040-e30ffe2050d6" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534004 1074 "First, notice that the left-hand side is a perfect square. It just needs to be factored:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9-24r+16{r}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {3}^{2}-2\\cdot 3\\cdot 4r+(4r)^{2} & =0 \\\\ (3-4r)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(3-4r)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{0} \\\\ |3-4r| & =0 \\\\ 3-4r & =0 \\\\ 4r & =3 \\\\ r & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534005 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533967 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534005 1069 "q-127236" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534005 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534005 1071 "Find the function $L(x),$ the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=4.$\n\n$L(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534005 1072 #uuid "51c7f8d6-81b7-561f-9aaa-77f7befa4a88" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534005 1074 "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=4$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(4)+{f}^{′}(4)(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have $f(4)=-5$ and ${f}^{′}(4)=-7,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =-5-7(x-4) \\\\  & =-5-7x+28 \\\\  & =-7x+23.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534006 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533854 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534006 1069 "q-556465" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534006 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534006 1071 "Estimate the value of $\\sqrt{28},$ rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\n$\\sqrt{28}≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534006 1072 #uuid "5297aedb-eec0-5c60-a99d-266da204b16b" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534006 1074 "To estimate $\\sqrt{28},$ we first find the two perfect squares closest to $28.$\n\nThe closest perfect squares are $25={5}^{2}$ and $36={6}^{2}.$\n\nSince $25<28<36,$ we know that the square root of $28$ lies between $5$ and $6.$\n\n![](images/9f/9f16093fe61b05142060e1dcc36cf824ec664b271499d24758831a6bc53757ac.png)\n\nSince $28$ lies between $25$ and $36,$ we expect $\\sqrt{28}$ to lie between $5$ and $6.$\n\nLet's test decimals near the middle by squaring them:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5.2}^{2} & =27.04 \\\\ {5.3}^{2} & =28.09\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that $28$ lies between $27.04$ and $28.09,$ so $5.2<\\sqrt{28}<5.3.$\n\nNext, we determine which value is closer to $28:$\n\n- The distance from $28$ to ${5.2}^{2}$ is $28-27.04=0.96.$\n\n- The distance from $28$ to ${5.3}^{2}$ is $28.09-28=0.09.$\n\nSince $28$ is much closer to $28.09,$ $\\sqrt{28}$ is closer to $5.3.$\n\nTherefore, rounded to $1$ decimal place, $\\sqrt{28}≈5.3.$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534007 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533894 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534007 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533925 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534007 1069 "q-355387" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534007 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534007 1071 "Let $B=\\{-4,-2,0,3\\}$ and suppose\n\n$$\nk(-4)=-5,\\,k(-2)=-8,\\,k(0)=-2,\\,k(3)=-8.\n$$\n\nCompute ${min}_{x\\in B}k(x)$ and $\\operatorname{arg}⁡{min}_{x\\in B}k(x).$\n\n$\\,$\n${min}_{x\\in B}k(x)=$ {{field-1}}\n\n$\\,$\n$\\operatorname{arg}⁡{min}_{x\\in B}k(x)=$ {{field-2}}" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534007 1072 #uuid "53479689-99f5-53fc-9bbf-170474b20c8c" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534007 1074 "Recall that \n\n- ${min}_{x\\in B}k(x)$ is the smallest output value, while\n\n- $\\operatorname{arg}⁡{min}_{x\\in B}k(x)$ is an input in $B$ where that smallest output occurs.\n\nThe output values are $-5,$ $-8,$ $-2,$ and $-8,$ so the minimum output value is $-8.$ This minimum occurs at both $x=-2$ and $x=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{min}_{x\\in B}k(x)=-8\\,\\text{and}\\,\\operatorname{arg}⁡{min}_{x\\in B}k(x)=\\{-2,3\\}.\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534008 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533962 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534008 1069 "q-269371" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534008 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534008 1071 "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 3}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=3({y}^{T}Ax)$ where \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534008 1072 #uuid "55449077-495e-5404-a705-ef8294705226" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534008 1074 "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWriting out $L$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\nL={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{3}3{a}_{ij}{y}_{i}{x}_{j},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}=3{y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${∇}_{A}L=3(y\\cdot {x}^{T}).$\n\nEvaluating at the given values $x=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=3([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & 7\\end{aligned}])=3([\\begin{aligned}-4 & 6 & 14 \\\\ 10 & -15 & -35\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 18 & 42 \\\\ 30 & -45 & -105\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534009 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533928 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534009 1069 "q-352380" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534009 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534009 1071 "Evaluate the series\n${\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}-6n+8}.$\nDo not round the answer.\n\n*Hint: Try to factor the denominator first.*\n\n${\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}-6n+8}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534009 1072 #uuid "5580c754-8067-5747-aaed-12ae4add5b01" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534009 1074 "We can factor the denominator and use partial fractions to show that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}-6n+8}=\\frac{1}{(n-4)(n-2)}=\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{n-4}-\\frac{1}{n-2}).\n$$\n\nSo, our series can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{n-4}-\\frac{1}{n-2})=2{\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }(\\frac{1}{n-4}-\\frac{1}{n-2}).\n$$\n\nLet's now compute the first few partial sums:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{5} & =2[(1-\\frac{1}{3})] \\\\ {s}_{6} & =2[(1-\\frac{1}{3})+(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{4})] \\\\ {s}_{7} & =2[(1-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}})+(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{4})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}}-\\frac{1}{5})] \\\\ {s}_{8} & =2[(1-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}})+(\\frac{1}{2}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}}-\\frac{1}{5})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}}-\\frac{1}{6})]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe notice that all of the terms cancel except $1,\\frac{1}{2},$ and the $-\\frac{1}{N-3}$ and  $-\\frac{1}{N-2}$ terms. Therefore, the $N$th partial sum is\n\n$$\n{s}_{N}=2(1+\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{N-3}-\\frac{1}{N-2}).\n$$\n\nFinally, we can determine the sum of the series by taking the limit of ${s}_{N}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}-6n+8} & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }{s}_{N} \\\\  & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }2(1+\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{N-3}-\\frac{1}{N-2}) \\\\  & =2(1+\\frac{1}{2}-0-0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534010 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533783 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534010 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533845 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534010 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533872 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534010 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533923 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534010 1069 "q-355224" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534010 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534010 1071 "| $t$ | $h(t)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $-5$ | $1$ |\n| $-3$ | $3$ |\n| $0$ | $-2$ |\n| $1$ | $5$ |\n| $2$ | $-4$ |\n\nThe table above gives values of a function $h$ on the set $T=\\{-5,-3,0,1,2\\}.$ Compute the following:\n\n${max}_{t\\in T}h(t)=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\operatorname{arg}⁡{max}_{t\\in T}h(t)=$\n{{field-2}}\n\n${min}_{t\\in T}h(t)=$\n{{field-3}}\n\n$\\operatorname{arg}⁡{min}_{t\\in T}h(t)=$\n{{field-4}}" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534010 1072 #uuid "5ce73dda-6fa0-5e6a-8244-c7f7fa67bacd" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534010 1074 "Recall that \n\n- ${max}_{x\\in S}f(x)$ is the maximum output value, while\n\n- $\\operatorname{arg}⁡{max}_{x\\in S}f(x)$ is an input in $S$ where that maximum output occurs.\n\nOn the other hand,\n\n- ${min}_{x\\in S}f(x)$ is the minimum output value, while\n\n- $\\operatorname{arg}⁡{min}_{x\\in S}f(x)$ is an input in $S$ where that minimum output occurs.\n\nThe output values are $1,3,-2,5,$ and $-4.$ The maximum output is $5$ (at $t=1$), and the minimum output is $-4$ (at $t=2$). \n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{max}_{t\\in T}h(t) & =5 \\\\ \\operatorname{arg}⁡{max}_{t\\in T}h(t) & =1 \\\\ {min}_{t\\in T}h(t) & =-4 \\\\ \\operatorname{arg}⁡{min}_{t\\in T}h(t) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534011 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533835 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534011 1069 "q-326521" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534011 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534011 1071 "There were approximately $28,\\,000$ factory jobs in the city in $2020.$  Economic forecasts predict that this number will grow by $35%$ by the year $2030.$  Which of the following is closest to the projected number of factory jobs in $2030?$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534011 1072 #uuid "5e32c9fa-0237-548e-8a15-6b3954405e5d" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534011 1074 "To find the projected number of jobs in $2030,$ we apply a $35%$ increase to the $2020$ figure.\n\nIncreasing by $35%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%+35%=135%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n135%=\\frac{135}{100}=1.35\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the new value by multiplying the original amount by $1.35:$\n\n$$\n28,\\,000\\times 1.35=37,\\,800\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projected number of factory jobs in $2030$ is $37,\\,800.$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534012 1068 #ed